# duration/CHANGELOG

## 20261021

0.11.0: ~ to_iso8601 writes a duration in its own terms, as the rest of the library does, rather than split into days, hours, minutes and seconds as in 0.10.0, which split: true still gives; + fractions to to_iso8601 and from_iso8601; + months to from_iso8601, into Months.

1. ~ Duration::ISO8601::Writer#to_iso8601: writes the duration in its own unit's designator as it is held, PT90M and P2W, a unit smaller than a second in seconds, where 0.10.0 wrote PT1H30M and P14D. Splitting was the one default in the library which threw the unit away.
2. + Duration::ISO8601::Writer#to_iso8601(split:): true splits into 0.10.0's days, hours, minutes and seconds; units given split into those, largest first. What remains after the last unit's whole part is written as a fraction upon it, where 0.10.0 refused any part second.
3. + Duration::ISO8601::Writer#to_iso8601(fraction_digits): rounds the last component to that many places, as Time#iso8601 does. Without it a fraction with no finite decimal raises ArgumentError; a Float is taken as it prints.
4. ~ Duration::ISO8601::Reader#from_iso8601: + a fraction upon the last component, with a full stop or a comma, as the standard allows.
5. ~ Duration::ISO8601::Reader#from_iso8601: + M before the T, read into Months alone, as the mean month. Any other unit raises ArgumentError upon it, as every unit still does upon Y.
6. ~ lib/Duration/Months.rb: the class comment, that from_iso8601 and to_iso8601 read and write M as the mean month.
7. ~ test/Duration/ISO8601_test.rb: #to_iso8601 expects each unit's own designator; + split: true, split: units, fractions, fraction digits, and months into Months.
8. ~ README.md: ISO 8601 rewritten for the above; + that the standard counts days and weeks as nominal, which here are 24 hours and seven days.
9. ~ Duration::VERSION: /0.10.0/0.11.0/


## 20261019

0.10.0: + to_iso8601 upon every unit, through Duration::ISO8601::Writer.

1. + Duration::ISO8601::Writer#to_iso8601: the duration as P#DT#H#M#S, leaving out what is zero, and PT0S where all of it is, each quantity taken whole through the units' own conversions. A part second or a negative duration raises ArgumentError. No W, as ISO 8601 allows weeks only alone or by its 2019 extension.
2. /Duration::ISO8601/Duration::ISO8601::Reader/: ISO8601 is now a namespace for the two mixins, as one module both extended and included would put every method upon both sides.
3. ~ Duration::Common: + include ISO8601::Writer.
4. ~ lib/Duration/{Nanoseconds,Microseconds,Milliseconds,Seconds,Minutes,Hours,Days,Weeks,Months}.rb: /extend ISO8601/extend ISO8601::Reader/
5. ~ lib/Duration/Months.rb: + to the class comment, that to_iso8601 writes the mean month out.
6. ~ lib/duration-classes.rb: /mixins rather than classes/modules rather than classes/
7. ~ test/Duration/ISO8601_test.rb: /describe Duration::ISO8601/describe Duration::ISO8601::Reader/; + Duration::ISO8601::Writer#to_iso8601; + that each method is upon its own side only.
8. ~ README.md: /Parsing ISO 8601/ISO 8601/, + to_iso8601 and the two mixins; ~ Naming the classes: /mixins rather than classes/modules rather than classes/.
9. ~ Duration::VERSION: /0.9.0/0.10.0/


## 20261018

0.9.0: + Duration::ISO8601, which gives every unit from_iso8601.

1. + lib/Duration/ISO8601.rb: from_iso8601 reads P#W#DT#H#M#S, any subset, into the unit of the class it extends, each component written being made in its own unit and summed into that one. Years and months raise ArgumentError, having no fixed length. A named constructor rather than a String accepted by new, so as no one format claims every string passed to new.
2. ~ lib/Duration/{Nanoseconds,Microseconds,Milliseconds,Seconds,Minutes,Hours,Days,Weeks,Months}.rb: + extend ISO8601.
3. ~ lib/Duration/Months.rb: + to the class comment, that from_iso8601 reads by the mean month and refuses P1M.
4. ~ lib/duration.rb: + autoload :ISO8601.
5. ~ lib/duration-classes.rb: + ISO8601 to the constants left where they are.
6. + test/Duration/ISO8601_test.rb
7. ~ test/loading_test.rb: + that from_iso8601 reads only the units a string names.
8. ~ test/duration-classes_test.rb: + ISO8601 to the constants left where they are.
9. ~ README.md: + Parsing ISO 8601; ~ Naming the classes: + ISO8601 to the constants left where they are, and /twelve constants/thirteen constants/ for a top-level include Duration; ~ Contributing: the fork link names the repository as renamed.
10. ~ duration.rb.gemspec: spec.license /Ruby/MIT/. LICENSE has been MIT since 0.3.0 added it.
